  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThrough tonight: continued warmer than normal and dry. The smokey \nconditions from the east into central Mississippi last evening have \nmostly dissipated. Surface ridging from the northeast will continue \nthrough tonight but gradually weaken. This will result in an east to \nsoutheast low level flow across our CWA that will continue to limit \nthe return of Gulf moisture. Our winds aloft will become \nsouthwesterly by afternoon though downstream of an approaching \nshortwave trough. This will work to increase moisture from the \nsouthwest and help our southwest zones top out in the lower 80s. \nTemperatures will top out in the mid to upper 70s elsewhere. /22/ \n\nSunday - Friday\n\nCooler temperatures and rain chances will usher in the new week as a \ncold front approaches the region. Once the front pushes into the \narea Sunday into Monday, rain chances will range between 15-65% with \nthe greatest chances in the MS Delta and along/north of I-20. \nAlthough rain chances will be present, rainfall amounts are expected \nto be limited (0.25 in or less) and won't relive drought conditions. \nPost-frontal passage will cool temperatures to below seasonal \naverages (5-10 degrees cooler) heading into the Thanksgiving \nholiday. A brief shortwave disturbance may bring an isolated (25% or \nless) shower or two in eastern MS on Wednesday but the Thanksgiving \nholiday and weekend is expected to be dry and quiet. /SW/",
